1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,962
Welcome to Module Twenty, and to the unit on the field notebook: retrieval and memory.

2
00:00:05,962 --> 00:00:07,962
You know the forager's dilemma.

3
00:00:07,962 --> 00:00:13,612
In a patchy landscape, every patch starts rich and gets poorer the longer you stay.

4
00:00:13,612 --> 00:00:21,262
Charnov's marginal value theorem says leave when your rate of gain in this patch drops below what you could expect elsewhere, counting the travel.

5
00:00:21,262 --> 00:00:23,850
Rich patches deserve long visits.

6
00:00:23,850 --> 00:00:26,625
Poor ones, a quick look and move on.

7
00:00:26,625 --> 00:00:30,062
An agent answering questions from documents is a forager.

8
00:00:30,062 --> 00:00:33,550
Its documents are cut into chunks, those are the patches.

9
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:35,512
A search engine ranks them.

10
00:00:35,512 --> 00:00:37,700
The agent works down the list.

11
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:41,200
And every chunk it reads costs context and attention.

12
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,812
The first few hits are usually the richest.

13
00:00:43,812 --> 00:00:48,050
At some point, reading one more chunk is worth less than it costs.

14
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:55,312
Fetching the right evidence before answering has a name: retrieval augmented generation, or RAG.

15
00:00:55,312 --> 00:01:01,225
And today, you'll use the source pack behind this very course as your first RAG system.

16
00:01:01,225 --> 00:01:05,187
The classic way to rank chunks is called B M twenty-five.

17
00:01:05,187 --> 00:01:09,900
It's keyword search, done cleverly, and it rests on three ideas.

18
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:13,850
First, rare words matter more than common ones.

19
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:20,225
A chunk that contains the word compaction is far more telling than one that contains the word agent.

20
00:01:20,225 --> 00:01:24,725
Second, repetition helps, but with diminishing returns.

21
00:01:24,725 --> 00:01:28,787
Five mentions beat one, but not by five times.

22
00:01:28,787 --> 00:01:34,337
Third, long chunks get a penalty, because they contain more words just by chance.

23
00:01:34,337 --> 00:01:40,812
Two dials control the last two ideas, and in today's widget you can turn them and watch the ranking change.

24
00:01:40,812 --> 00:01:43,875
You might expect keyword search to be old-fashioned.

25
00:01:43,875 --> 00:01:50,375
But one of today's sources found that none of the eleven coding agents it studied use embedding search to find code.

26
00:01:50,375 --> 00:01:56,312
They use plain text search, and it works, because code and technical writing are full of exact names.

27
00:01:56,312 --> 00:01:59,075
The other big decision is chunk size.

28
00:01:59,075 --> 00:02:03,900
Too large, and each chunk covers several topics, so the right one is hard to pick out.

29
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:06,900
Too small, and the idea gets cut in half.

30
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,637
Overlapping chunks a little helps with the cut.

31
00:02:09,637 --> 00:02:12,412
Here's where the foraging metaphor fails.

32
00:02:12,412 --> 00:02:13,900
A forager learns.

33
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:17,725
It remembers which patches paid off and changes its behaviour.

34
00:02:17,725 --> 00:02:20,625
B M twenty-five learns nothing at all.

35
00:02:20,625 --> 00:02:23,700
The same question gets the same ranking forever.

36
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:25,950
And it doesn't know what words mean.

37
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:33,912
Ask about an agent forgetting its earlier rules, and it may never find the passage about compaction, because the words don't match.

38
00:02:33,912 --> 00:02:42,875
In the lab, you'll write ten questions about things you've learned in this course, run them through the pack's search tool, and judge the top five results yourself.

39
00:02:42,875 --> 00:02:48,750
That gives you a number, precision at five, and a little test set that you'll reuse for the rest of the unit.

40
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:53,700
Then your coding agent will try different dial settings to see whether anything beats the default.

41
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:56,450
Open the widget, and start foraging.

